## From a sketch to a mechanism

In [the lesson on the value ladder](../../11-copywriting/10-ladder/) you built its rungs: a lead magnet, a low-priced first offer, a core offer, a one-time offer. That was a sketch on paper. To make it work you need a **mechanism**: pages people can come to, a form they can send, an email that arrives, and numbers that show what is going on. In this lesson we assemble it from what you can already do: the form and consents ([the lesson on email](../02-email/)), the flow ([the lesson on automation](../01-automation/)) and the honesty of the rungs (the lesson on the ladder).

There is no new law and no new trick here, only **assembly**. So this lesson is about discipline: complicate nothing, hide nothing and count everything.

## Seven parts of the mechanism

1. **The lead-magnet page.** One goal: the person leaves an email address and gets the material. No menu, no five other calls to action, a headline that names the benefit (the four U’s from [the lesson on benefits and 4U](../../11-copywriting/03-benefit/)) and a short form.
2. **The form.** One field: email. Two empty boxes: for processing the address and for receiving news (the email lesson). The button says what the person will get: “Get the checklist”, not “Submit”.
3. **Confirmation.** The person gets an email with a “confirm your address” link. If there is no confirmation (it happens with very simple forms), decide that deliberately: without it, strangers’ addresses and typos get onto the list more easily.
4. **Delivery.** Hand the material over **at once**: a link on the thank-you page and a duplicate email. If a person confirmed their address and no material came, you lose their trust for good.
5. **The thank-you page.** The most underrated page on a site. Only people who have just done something open it, so they are attentive and well disposed.
6. **Follow-up.** A short cascade from the first lesson’s flow: remind, prove, offer, with pauses and an exit condition.
7. **Counting.** The events by which you’ll later count what happens at each rung.

## The lead magnet: what to give and how

We spoke about choosing the material in the part on copywriting: one narrow question of your audience. Now about the delivery.

- **Format.** For a small project a one- or two-page PDF, a page on the site or a series of three emails is enough. The PDF must open on a phone and weigh no more than a few megabytes.
- **Storage.** Best of all, put the material on your own site and give a link. A file attached to an email is often blocked by mail filters.
- **No surprises.** What you promised on the page must be in the file. “A checklist of ten questions” is ten questions, not three and an advertisement.
- **Value without a sequel.** A person who read the checklist and never came back still got what they came for. That is the main rule of an honest rung.

## The thank-you page: what it needs

This page needs four things, in this order:

1. **Confirmation** of what happened: “The checklist has been sent to your email”.
2. **The benefit itself**: a “Download” link (even if the email has already gone).
3. **What happens next**: “Over the next week I’ll send two more emails, and you can unsubscribe in either”.
4. **One next step**: either the main action from the brief (“Book a consultation”) or a one-time offer.

A **one-time offer (OTO)** on this page is honest if it is:

- **related to what was just received**: after a checklist, “a review of your situation”, not a random service;
- **with a visible price**, which won’t change tomorrow if you promise so;
- **with a decline button** that is neither hidden nor accusing (“No, I don’t want to save money” is manipulation, called confirmshaming);
- **without invented deadlines.** If the deadline is real, give it as a date; if not, there is none. In the US the FTC treats false urgency as deceptive, and the EU’s list of banned practices names claiming an offer is available only for a very limited time when it is not (Directive 2005/29/EC, Annex I).

On payment: if your first offer is paid, taking money is a question not for a marketer but for an accountant and a lawyer (terms of sale, receipts, sales tax, refunds, and in the EU and UK a right to withdraw from an online purchase within 14 days). Find out how this works for your form of business before launch, not after the first payment.

## Events: what to count and what to call them

To count later, every rung has to be marked with an **event**, that is, a record that “this happened”. The rules are simple:

- **object_action**, in Latin letters, lowercase, joined by underscores: `lead_form_view`, `lead_form_submit`, `lead_confirmed`, `oto_accept`.
- **One name, one action**, for good. If you rename it, the old and new statistics won’t join up.
- **No personal data in the name.** An event must not contain a person’s email or name. In the part on metrics you’ll put these events into a counter, and we’ll talk about the ethics of measuring separately.

## The arithmetic of the ladder

Say fifteen hundred people a month come to the lead-magnet page. Some share leave an email, some confirm, then a few buy the low-priced offer and some of them order the core service. Open the diagram and move the rates. Beside each number the diagram shows a **plausible range**: the limits within which it swings by chance alone. With small numbers the range is huge.

<LadderMath />

What follows for practice:

- **The lower rungs are always small.** If three people a month order the core service, a month with five doesn’t prove the page got better.
- **Count rung by rung, not all together.** The share who left an email is of the page’s visitors, the share who bought is of those who confirmed. Mixing denominators gives pretty but meaningless numbers (more in the lesson on metrics that mislead).
- **Quality beats quantity.** A lead magnet that collected a thousand addresses and not one client failed. Look not at “how many subscribed” but at “how many of the subscribers reached the main action”.

<details>
<summary>Check yourself: why does a lead-magnet form ask only for an email address?</summary>

Sending the material by email needs nothing else. Every extra field lowers the share who complete the form, and data you didn’t collect doesn’t have to be stored, protected or deleted at a person’s request.

</details>

## What can go wrong

- **The material is weaker than the promise.** People will unsubscribe and tell others.
- **The email with the material went to spam.** Check the SPF and DKIM records (the email lesson) and open the email yourself from another address.
- **The offer is irritating.** You put three buttons and a timer on the thank-you page. Leave one button and one step.
- **The count doesn’t add up.** If more people “confirmed” than “left an email”, the event is in the wrong place. Check, in order, where it fires.

This lesson is general information, not legal advice. Rules on consent, pricing and consumer withdrawal differ by country and state, so check the current texts and, for a real launch, ask a lawyer or your consumer authority (the FTC in the US, the CMA and Trading Standards in the UK, your national consumer authority in the EU).

## Practice

Build the ladder mechanism in the note.

1. **The lead-magnet page.** The name of the material, a one-field form (email), two unchecked boxes and a button.
2. **The thank-you page.** A link to the material, a link to the main action from the brief and a one-time offer with the price as a number, an accept button and a “No, thanks” button. If the brief has services, relate the offer to one of them.
3. **Events.** At least four names in Latin letters joined by underscores.

The check looks at form: one email field, two unchecked boxes, a price as a number, a decline, event names. Whether the ladder is honest is for you to decide: go through the six rules of an honest ladder from the previous part and make sure that at every rung the person gets something of use even if they go no further.
